From: =?UTF-8?q?Martin=20=C5=BDdila?= <m.zdila@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 12:43:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] expose pt.blocked_route_types on /route-pt, as
 PtIsochroneResource already does

The Request object has carried blockedRouteTypes for years and all three
PtRouter implementations honour it -- it is passed straight into GraphExplorer,
and PtRouterFreeWalkImpl and PtRouterTripBasedImpl both read it from the
request. PtIsochroneResource exposes it as `pt.blocked_route_types`. Only
PtRouteResource never wired it up, so a caller can exclude a mode from an
isochrone but not from a journey, which looks like an oversight rather than a
position: the parameter already has a name, a default and an implementation.

Four lines, no new concepts, and the same name and default as the isochrone
resource so the two endpoints stay consistent.

Two tests on the existing sample feed, whose NADAV -> NANAA connection is served
only by route_type 3: blocking bit 3 leaves no transit leg (a walking path may
still come back, which is why the assertion is on leg type rather than on the
absence of a path), and blocking bit 2 leaves the bus connection untouched, so
the filter is selective rather than a blunt off switch.

Boarding penalties are deliberately not exposed here. Request also carries
boardingPenaltiesByRouteType, but that is a Map<Integer, Long> and needs a query
parameter format nobody has agreed on, which is a separate discussion from
wiring up a parameter that already exists.

